      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the new class mean, we need to calculate the total score of all students and then divide by the total number of students.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total score for the initial group of 30 students. The mean score for these students was 72%, so their total score is:\n\\[ 30 \\times 72 = 2160 \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the total score for the remaining 6 students. The mean score for these students was 78%, so their total score is:\n\\[ 6 \\times 78 = 468 \\]\n\nNow, we add the total scores of both groups to get the overall total score for the class:\n\\[ 2160 + 468 = 2628 \\]\n\nThe total number of students in the class is 36. Therefore, the new class mean is the overall total score divided by the total number of students:\n\\[ \\frac{2628}{36} = 73 \\]\n\nSo, the new class mean is:\n\\[ \\boxed{73} \\]\n\nGiven that function $f(x)$ reaches its minimum value of $1$ at $x=0$, and $f(x) \\leq kx$ always holds true in the interval $[1,2]$, the maximum value of the real number $k$ is ______.\nGiven that the function \\( f(x) \\) reaches its minimum value of 1 at \\( x = 0 \\), we know that \\( f(0) = 1 \\). Also, the inequality \\( f(x) \\leq kx \\) must hold true for all \\( x \\) in the interval \\([1, 2]\\).\n\nTo find the maximum value of \\( k \\), we need to compare \\( f(0) \\) with the maximum value of the function \\( f(x) \\) on the interval \\([1, 2]\\), because \\( f(x) \\leq kx \\) implies that the value of \\( f(x) \\) at the boundary of the interval \\([1, 2]\\) (i.e., \\( f(1) \\) or \\( f(2) \\)) should not exceed \\( kx \\) for all \\( x \\) in \\([1, 2]\\).\n\nSince \\( f(0) = 1 \\), we consider the value of \\( f(1) \\) or \\( f(2) \\) to be the least upper bound of \\( f(x) \\) on \\([1, 2]\\). If \\( f(1) < k \\cdot 1 = k \\), then \\( f(2) \\) can be at most \\( k \\) (because \\( k )\\) without violating the inequality. This means \\( f(1) \\leq k \\) and \\( f(2) \\leq 2k \\).\n\nSince \\( f(x) \\) is continuous and strictly decreasing on \\([0, 1]\\), and strictly increasing on \\([0, 2]\\), we have:\n\\[ f(1) < k \\quad \\text{and} \\quad f(2) \\leq 2k. \\]\n\nTo find \\( k \\), we need to find the largest \\( k \\) such that \\( f(x) \\) is always less than or equal to \\( kx \\). Since \\( f(0) = 1 \\), we must have:\n\\[ f(x) \\leq kx \\quad \\text{for all} \\quad x \\in [1, 2]. \\]\nThis means \\( k \\) must be greater than or equal to \\( f(0) = 1 \\). To ensure that \\( f(x) \\leq kx \\) holds for all \\( x \\) in \\([1, 2]\\), the maximum value of \\( f(x) \\) on \\([1, 2]\\) must be less than or equal to \\( 2k \\).\n\nSince \\( f(1) < k \\), the maximum value of \\( f(1) \\) is less than \\( k \\). Also, since \\( f(2) \\leq 2k \\), the maximum value of \\( f(2) \\) is less than or equal to \\( 2k \\). To ensure these conditions are met for all \\( x \\) in \\([1, 2]\\), we need:\n\\[ k \\leq 2. \\]\n\nThus, the maximum value of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2}. \\]\n\nA person buys an article at Rs. 500. At what price should he sell the article so as to make a profit of 20%?\nTo determine the selling price for a 20% profit, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the profit amount.\n2. Add the profit amount to the cost price to get the selling price.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the profit amount.\nThe cost price of the article is Rs. 500. The profit percentage is 20%.\n\nThe profit amount is calculated as:\n\\[ \\text{Profit} = \\text{Cost Price} \\times \\frac{\\text{Profit Percentage}}{100} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Profit} = 500 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 500 \\times 0.20 = 100 \\]\n\nStep 2: Add the profit amount to the cost price to get the selling price.\n\\[ \\text{Selling Price} = \\text{Cost Price} + \\text{Profit} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Selling Price} = 500 + 100 = 600 \\]\n\nTherefore, the selling price should be \\boxed{600}.",
